WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.344
The experiment log.

2
00:00:01.624 --> 00:00:09.795
Every run of the harness writes one row to an experiment log, and the log is what makes your results reproducible and your write-up honest.

3
00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:26.203
A row holds: a run id and timestamp; the git commit; the dataset name and version, and which split; the system configuration (chunker and its parameters, embedding model, retriever, k, reranker, generator model id, effort setting, prompt version);

4
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:44.552
the judge model id and prompt version, with the TPR and TNR used for correction; every metric with its interval and n; total cost and median latency; and a one-line hypothesis written before the run (“a reranker will raise recall@5 without hurting latency much”),

5
00:00:44.832 --> 00:00:45.984
plus the outcome.

6
00:00:46.264 --> 00:00:52.728
A JSONL file in the repository is enough, with a short script that prints the last few runs as a table.

7
00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:58.085
A spreadsheet works too, as long as rows are written by the harness, not typed by hand.

8
00:00:58.365 --> 00:01:05.256
The log also protects you from the most common way eval-driven development goes wrong: overfitting to the eval set.

9
00:01:05.536 --> 00:01:14.795
If you try twenty variants and keep the best dev-set score, the winning score is biased upward, because some of its margin is luck on those particular questions.

10
00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:31.843
Defenses: the held-out set, run rarely; a log that shows how many variants you tried (so the write-up can say so); refreshing the dev set with new production failures so it keeps resembling reality; and reading a sample of the changed outputs for every claimed improvement,

11
00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:35.707
to confirm the gain is real behavior and not a judge quirk.
